So to tackle the "update the data" case. What we could do is this:

``` js
template: function(data, hoganTemplateFn) {
  var newData = data.map()..
  return hoganTemplateFn(data)
}
```

So the "trick" is to send the default hogan templating function when we get a function as a template. This way the user can reformat the data and still use the default hogan templating system.

Thoughts @algolia/javascript ?

@pixelastic 

> Maybe just wait for the need on other widgets before merging?

I have one! Right now, I'm using an optional  `displayFunction` for my facets. It is actually only used in one case of weirdly formatted data : price ranges, but could be needed with other types of attributes.

I'm not found of modifying the data within the `template` function, because to me we should just expect a template to be returned here, not potential data modification.

On the other hand, I really like the `transformData` idea of a callback to call just before the template rendering. We're always passing an object when rendering a Hogan template, so we would always have the same prototype.

To me, this:

``` js
{
  transformData: function (hit) {
    return { name: hit.first_name + ' ' + hit.last_name };
  },
  template: '{{ name }}'
}
```

makes more sense than this:

``` js
{
  template: function(hit, mustacheTemplateFn) {
    return mustacheTemplateFn('{{ name }}', { name: hit.first_name + ' ' + hit.last_name; });
  }
}
```

